          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"“I love you, Your Majesty.”\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her aside independently characterizes that love as greater than she can express, reinforcing the avowal’s reporting force without determining its real-world sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The avowal is explicitly framed as fulfilment of Cordelia’s existing filial bond rather than as a new promise. Cordelia invokes the duties she owes as Lear’s daughter—obedience, love, and honour—and presents herself as returning them. Lear does not accept this as an adequate response to his demand; he tells her to amend it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured avowal qualified by duty and precise limits, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged verbal response. Cordelia describes difficulty verbalizing her heart, but the resulting statement remains controlled and propositional.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial duty, the expression functions as a restrained refusal to exaggerate or compete in Lear’s public love test. “No more nor less” limits the claim and implicitly rejects the demanded superlative rhetoric.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare which loves him most while distributing portions of his kingdom. After her sisters make expansive declarations, Cordelia initially answers “Nothing,” then gives this qualified avowal. Lear treats it as inadequate and urges her to revise it to protect her fortunes.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes, rather than newly creates, her filial bond: as Lear’s daughter she owes and returns fitting obedience, love, and honour. She also limits that obligation, explaining that marriage would transfer part of her love, care, and duty to her husband. Lear does not accept the statement as sufficient for his love test and asks her to amend it.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is from William Shakespeare’s King Lear, Act 1, Scene 1.\",\"limitations\":\"The assessment is confined to the supplied scene. It supports reading Cordelia’s affection as present through her aside and explanation, but it does not by itself establish every aspect of her prior relationship with Lear or subsequent conduct.\"}"
        }
